From: CarPlay C0 lane (2026-11-22)
Subject: 0518 sweet_nub: print the object the NCM/Mux clients dispatch through

The clients forward endpoint requests to ctl+0x110 / ctl+0x120 and then call slot 29 of that
object's vtable with a tag (census in iface-forwarders.json). Which object that is cannot be
decided statically -- the vtable is loaded from the instance -- and implementing C1's endpoint
virtuals from the tag numbers alone would be a guess. This prints both pointers, the vtable
they carry, and slot 29, so one boot pins down the dispatcher C1 must satisfy.

Prepared while waiting for the device lock on the night of 2026-11-22, after the 0517 fallback
fix; the dereference is guarded by the kernel text range so a NULL or non-text pointer is
reported instead of faulting the probe.

--- a/libkern/c++/sweet_nub.cpp
+++ b/libkern/c++/sweet_nub.cpp
@@ -273,6 +273,27 @@
 		}
 	}
 	printf("SWEET NUB: children %d\n", children);
+
+	/*
+	 * 0518: the NCM/Mux clients dispatch their endpoint requests through the objects at
+	 * ctl+0x110 / ctl+0x120, and slot 29 of whatever vtable those objects carry is the tag
+	 * dispatcher C1 has to satisfy. Static analysis cannot identify that vtable (it is read
+	 * from the instance and the controllers are built at runtime), and inventing endpoint
+	 * semantics from the tag numbers alone would be a guess. One printed line pins it.
+	 * The pointers may legitimately be NULL in the direct-create path -- that is information,
+	 * so print them unconditionally and only dereference a pointer that looks like text.
+	 */
+	for (unsigned off = 0x110; off <= 0x120; off += 0x10) {
+		void *obj = *(void **)((char *)ctl + off);
+		printf("SWEET NUB: ctl+0x%x = %p", off, obj);
+		if (obj != NULL) {
+			void **vt = *(void ***)obj;
+			if (vt != NULL && (uint64_t)vt >= SWEET_NUB_KERNEL_BASE) {
+				printf(", vtable %p, slot29 %p", vt, vt[29]);
+			}
+		}
+		printf("\n");
+	}
 
